33:| 2026-09-23 | **BEM rotor sizing used a standalone circular disc instead of the ring annulus, oversizing the blade span** | Island 1 (3 rotors) carries **14.6253 kg** of blade mass against island 3's (1 rotor) 2.9735 kg. Its top rotor's span is **1.9584 m**, sweeping 36.93 m2 where 27.68 m2 was required. The top-heavy head then buckles the lower transmission. CORRECTED 2026-09-23: this row previously read 11.54 kg, 380 %, and pinned M proportional to 1/sqrt(N) | `objective_v10.jl` sets `span = 0.75*r_rotor_i*blade_scale_i`, where `BEM.rotor_radius_for_power` returns a SOLID DISC radius, so the ring radius never enters the span. Three further defects compound it: `power_split = 0.6` puts 60 % on the top rotor, the `n_active == 1` branch sizes a single rotor at 0.6*P, and the unanchored 50 m shear reference de-rates the whole machine. CORRECTED: the wake factor does not invert the shear, and the slender hub does not enter the span at all | **OPEN / DOCUMENTED, with corrected attribution.** Blueprint in `handovers/handover-2026-09-23-multirotor-bem-sizing.md`, which now carries a verified-correction banner. Measured cumulative factors on the top rotor: annulus 2.17x, equal share 5.10x, inflow 17.30x. Note Jamieson's 1/sqrt(N) is the DISC law and is the WRONG guard for a TRPT: ring-anchored blades fall nearer 1/N^2, measured 0.128 for N=3. The sizing-to-dynamics hand-off is verified correct |
34:| 2026-09-23 | **Island 1 structural bottleneck is lower transmission Euler buckling, driven by inverted-pendulum blade mass** | Island 1 hits dynamic FoS trough 1.4772 at t=0.82 s and limit cycles at 1.79 m p2p, while Island 3 passes cleanly at FoS 12.5 | Bottleneck isolated to Ring 4 (GID 31) in lower transmission cylinder: failure is 100% axial column buckling (w_A = 0.6767, P_crit = 1621.5 N). Expansion rings (FoS 3.35, 10.48) and hub (FoS 6.37) are sound. Optimizer shrank r_hub to 2.61 m, blowing blade span to 1.96 m and hub blade mass to 9.46 kg (3.2x Island 3) via m ∝ span³. Top-heavy 14.6 kg head whips slender 12 mm cylinder. | **CLOSED.** Evaluated via `scratch/probe_ring_fos_breakdown.jl` and `scratch/probe_mass_resonance.jl`. Uniform bare mass column collapses lateral limit cycle by 3.1x (0.57 m p2p), cone stays taut at 230–283 N, and FoS trough surges to 6.0522. Island 1 is an unviable, degenerate genome; multi-rotor physics model is verified sound. |
38:| 2026-09-22 | **Expansion-rotor translational mass never reached the ring node**. | On island 1 rings 8 and 9 read `mass = 1.5702 kg`, the bare ring. They also carried `er.mass` 2.547 and 2.613 kg, and rotary inertia `I_z` 31.9055 and 32.4947 kg m2. Their lateral sensitivity `a = F/m` was about 2.6x too high. The rotary inertia `J_rotor` was added on 2026-08-25. The physical mass was not. | `mass_node += er.mass` in `_build_kite_turbine_system_impl`, always. It is deliberately NOT gated on `EXPANSION_PHYSICS[].blade_inertia`. The mass is real either way, and `expansion_airborne_mass` already budgets it for lifter sizing. So the ODE node weighed less than the lifter was sized against. `er.mass` is the ASSEMBLY total. Add it once, never times `n_blades` (`test_blade_mass_law.jl` guards that). Rings 8 and 9 now read 4.1175 and 4.1836 kg. Island 3 has no expansion rotors and is unchanged bit-for-bit. `57b3f58`. |
61:| 2026-08-24 | **~~Settle-vs-ODE 94× twist mismatch~~ → RETRACTED 2026-08-25: it was `J·θ`, not a rope-torque gap** | 2-rotor seed settles then reverses to ω<0. A first pass reported the ODE rope torque as 2.36 N·m vs the 221 N·m of the bisection ("94×") | The "2.36 N·m" figure came from `torques[1]` after the generator reaction subtraction (a net residual, ~1% convergence error). The chord model and ODE rope chain actually agree to 5 figures. The real cause is `ring_forces.jl:314`: `torques[ri] -= J_rotor·alpha[ri]` used the twist ANGLE θ as angular acceleration. A spurious torsional spring (J·θ) that braked the machine to reversal. Only fires when expansion rotors exist (why 1-rotor works, 2-rotor dies) | Blade mass is now rotary INERTIA (added to ring `inertia_z` at build, `dω/dt=τ/(I_z+J)`). J·θ term removed. Also fixed: preload used 3D chord as axial gap (over-tensioned 18 to 22×), bisection negative-twist floor |

== 95c385d body ==
test(wind): pin one wind authority — the shear exponent comes from the site spec

The D1 landing fixed the ODE and decoder paths. Three shipped wind closures
still hand-wrote `(z / h_ref)^(1/7)`: sim_runner.jl (6), visualization.jl (8),
control_map_hunt.jl (3). They agree with the anchor site by coincidence, so they
pass every behaviour test today and would silently keep flying the anchor
exponent once `at_site` re-bases a machine on a site whose exponent is not 1/7.

- src/wind_profile.jl: add `site_shear(h_ref, h)`, the single place the runtime
  shear exponent is written down (reads SITE_ANCHOR.shear_exp). Export it.
- retire all 17 literals to `site_shear(p.h_ref, z)` (and `p_st.h_ref`), which is
  numerically identical at the anchor.
- test/test_wind_authority.jl: behaviour pins + a COUNTED source pin, wired into
  the fast suite. RED-first evidence: with the literals restored, the source pin
  fails (`count("site_shear(", flat) >= n` -> 0 >= 6) while all 23 behaviour
  checks still pass — which is exactly why a source assertion is required.


